    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "The two-step machine"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "First machine doubles a number. Second machine adds 3. Put 5 in: 10 comes out of the first, then 13 from the second. Joining machines this way is called composition."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "g o f",
          "right to left",
          "g(f(x))",
          "not commutative",
          "associative",
          "identity",
          "inverse",
          "domain check"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "If f: A to B and g: B to C, the composition g o f maps x to g(f(x)). We do f first, then g.\nRead g o f as g of f. The rightmost function acts first.\nExample: f(x) = 2x and g(x) = x + 3. Then (g o f)(x) = g(2x) = 2x + 3.\nBut (f o g)(x) = f(x + 3) = 2(x + 3) = 2x + 6, which is different. So composition is not commutative.\nComposition is associative: (h o g) o f = h o (g o f), so brackets do not change the result.\nThe identity function I(x) = x leaves any function unchanged: f o I = I o f = f.\nIf f o g = g o f = I, then g is the inverse of f, written f^-1.\nCheck the domains: the output of the first function must lie in the domain of the second."
        }
      },
